Welcome to Pod DB.

This repository contains sources for Pod a high performance real-time database.
Works across multiple channels (Web, and Raw TCP).

Regards,
Kabeer Jaffri


===============================================================================

Roadmap:

- [done] Create working database which is real-time fetchable via Websockets
- [done] Namespaces (scopes): every connection is confined to one namespace
- [done] Custom secondary indexes (composite, array-contains)
- [done] Snapshotting and delta resolution
- [done] CEL rules engine + HS256 token authentication
- [done] Write-ahead commit log, resume tokens and delta resume
- [done] Pods cluster router (namespace sharding over a consistent-hash ring)
- [done] Node SDK (packages/node)
- Online resharding / data migration when cluster membership changes
- Replication

===============================================================================

Layout:

src/storage     StorageDriver interface + LevelDB driver (atomic batches, prefix scans)
src/db          Database engine: paths, namespaces, key layout, documents,
                field paths, query evaluation, CRUD / QUERY / MUTATE_MANY
src/realtime    Subscriptions: snapshots, ordered deltas, resume tokens
src/rules       CEL interpreter, HS256 JWT, security rules / authorizer
src/server      Configuration, connection authentication, outbound registry
src/cluster     Router <-> node link framing, TCP links, consistent-hash ring
src/network/ws  Wire protocol parsing and request handlers
src/entry       Database node (standalone.cpp) and pods router (router.cpp)
tests/unit      C++ unit tests (pod_tests)
tests/e2e       End-to-end protocol tests (bun)
bench           Load generator / benchmark (bun)
examples/web    Pod Board, a real-time collaborative example app

===============================================================================

Running:

Build requires vcpkg configured and $VCPKG_ROOT configured, and a C++23
compiler (GCC 13+).
cmake -B build -S . -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE="$VCPKG_ROOT/scripts/buildsystems/vcpkg.cmake"
cmake --build build --parallel

./build/pod
Clients connect to ws://localhost:3000/webchannel?ns=<namespace>

Configuration is read from the environment; every variable is documented in
.env.example.

Security: POD_RULES_PATH=rules.json POD_AUTH_SECRET=... ./build/pod
  Clients pass an HS256 JWT as ?t=<token>; see protocol/README.

Cluster (two nodes behind a router):
  POD_PORT=3001 POD_INTERNAL_PORT=4001 POD_STORAGE_PATH=./n1 POD_CLUSTER_SECRET=$S ./build/pod
  POD_PORT=3002 POD_INTERNAL_PORT=4002 POD_STORAGE_PATH=./n2 POD_CLUSTER_SECRET=$S ./build/pod
  POD_ROLE=router POD_PORT=3000 POD_CLUSTER_SECRET=$S \
    POD_NODES=n1=127.0.0.1:4001,n2=127.0.0.1:4002 ./build/pod
  Clients connect to the router exactly as to a single node.

Development loop:
watchexec -e cpp,h -r -- "cmake --build build --parallel --verbose && ./build/pod"

===============================================================================

Testing:

cmake --build build --target pod_tests && ./build/pod_tests
cd tests/e2e && bun install && POD_BIN=../../build/pod bun test
  (or: tests/e2e/run-external.sh ./build/pod)
tests/e2e/run-cluster.sh ./build/pod   (router, auth and rules suite)

Benchmark:

bench/run.sh ./build/pod bun bench/pod-bench.ts
  CONNECTIONS, DEPTH, DURATION_MS and SUBSCRIBERS tune the load.

Example app:

bench/run.sh ./build/pod bun examples/web/demo.ts      (headless walkthrough)
cd examples/web && bun run dev                         (browser UI)

===============================================================================

Client SDK (packages/node):

@pod-db/node is the TypeScript client for Node, Bun and browsers: documents,
queries, mutateMany, real-time listeners with resume across reconnects, index
management and typed errors. See packages/node/README.md.
cd packages/node && bun install && bun run typecheck && bun run test
POD_URL=ws://127.0.0.1:3000/webchannel bun run test:integration  (running server)
